    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "How did you sleep last night? If you’re like most people, you choose your favourite position at bedtime. But once sleep set in, you likely changed to different positions."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "If you wonder whether there’s a best way to sleep, you’re not alone. There’s plenty of advice online about how to sleep. Many devices have been developed to help you sleep in a "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b1",
            "number": 1
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " position."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "But it turns out we have far less "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b2",
            "number": 2
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " over our sleep positions than we might think. According to some sleep scientists, we might be better off not even trying."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "“Sometimes there’s more harm than good in "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b3",
            "number": 3
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " to control your sleep position,” says Raman Malhotra. He is a past president of the American Academy of Sleep Medicine."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Joseph De Koninck, a psychologist in Canada, has wondered how people really sleep at night. He created a way to "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b4",
            "number": 4
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " sleep positions without waking a patient. As it turned out, “the position you take at the beginning of sleep is not "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "necessarily",
            "ref": "v-necessarily"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " the one you use through the night.”"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "i1",
        "type": "imageRef",
        "image": "img1"
      },
      {
        "id": "p6",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "After dozens of studies, De Koninck found one thing: Once you go to sleep, your body is "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b5",
            "number": 5
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " its position."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p7",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Malhotra agrees. “There are "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "definitely",
            "ref": "v-definitely"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " positions that are more "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b6",
            "number": 6
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " for certain patients,” he says. For example, an injury in your left leg might cause you to sleep mostly on your right side. But it can be hard to "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b7",
            "number": 7
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " people to avoid certain sleep positions."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p8",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Most patients who need to change their sleep position are back sleepers experiencing sleep "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "apnea",
            "zh": "呼吸暂停"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". In this case, a person may stop breathing several times per night."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p9",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Some people "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b8",
            "number": 8
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " sleep training devices to keep them off their backs. Others may simply tape a tennis ball to their back. However, patients often give up tennis balls or other devices "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b9",
            "number": 9
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " the "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "discomfort",
            "ref": "v-discomfort"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " they cause."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p10",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Instead of worrying about something hard to control, Malhotra says it’s worth asking yourself how you feel when you "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b10",
            "number": 10
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". Overall, he says, if you’re well-rested, you don’t need to worry about sleep positions. So next time you go to sleep, close your eyes, relax, and let nature take its course."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
